  "name": null,
  "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
